    summary: "A famous eight-pin chip that makes timed pulses and steady square waves using just a capacitor and a couple of resistors.",
    purpose: "The 555 has been in production since 1972 and is still one of the best-selling chips ever, because it does two jobs with almost no extra parts. As an astable oscillator it produces a continuous square wave, perfect for blinking LEDs, beeping buzzers or generating a PWM signal. As a monostable (one-shot) timer it produces a single pulse of fixed length after a trigger, such as keeping a light on for 30 seconds after a button press. It runs from 4.5 V to 16 V and its output can source or sink up to about 200 mA, enough to drive LEDs, small relays or a transistor directly. It is a great first chip for learning timing.",
    how: "Inside are two comparators, a flip-flop (a one-bit memory) and a discharge transistor. A built-in chain of three equal resistors sets reference points at one-third and two-thirds of the supply voltage. An external capacitor charges through resistors; when its voltage reaches two-thirds of the supply (the THRESHOLD pin), the flip-flop flips, the output goes low and the discharge transistor empties the capacitor. When the TRIGGER pin falls below one-third, the flip-flop flips back and the output goes high. Since both thresholds are fractions of the supply, timing barely changes as the battery runs down. Astable frequency ≈ 1.44 ÷ ((R1 + 2·R2) × C); monostable pulse ≈ 1.1 × R × C.",
    integration: "Pinout: 1 GND, 2 TRIGGER, 3 OUTPUT, 4 RESET, 5 CONTROL, 6 THRESHOLD, 7 DISCHARGE, 8 VCC. Tie RESET (pin 4) to VCC unless you use it, put 10 nF from CONTROL (pin 5) to ground for stability, and decouple VCC with 100 nF plus 10 µF, because the bipolar NE555 draws a large current spike every time the output switches. For a basic astable, connect R1 from VCC to pin 7, R2 from pin 7 to pins 6 and 2 joined, and the timing capacitor from there to ground. Keep resistors between about 1 kΩ and 1 MΩ. The output high level is about 1.7 V below VCC, so on 5 V it gives roughly 3.3 V.",
