    how: "On the input side is an infrared LED; on the output side, facing it across a clear insulating gap, is a phototransistor, a transistor whose base is driven by light instead of a wire. Current through the LED makes light, and the light lets current flow through the phototransistor from collector to emitter. The ratio of output current to LED current is the current transfer ratio (CTR): for the PC817 it is 50–600 % depending on the grade letter (A, B, C, D), and it falls with age and temperature. Phototransistors are slow, with switching times of several microseconds and much longer with large load resistors, so a PC817 is good for signals up to roughly 10–20 kHz.",
    integration: "PC817 pinout: 1 anode, 2 cathode (input LED); 3 emitter, 4 collector (output). Drive the LED through a series resistor: R = (V_drive − 1.2 V) ÷ I_F, aiming for 5–10 mA, e.g. 330 Ω from 5 V or 220 Ω from 3.3 V. On the output, connect the emitter to the receiving side's ground and a pull-up resistor (4.7–10 kΩ) from collector to that side's supply; the collector then goes low when the LED is on. Each side has its own supply and ground, and they must stay separate, or there is no isolation. Add a reverse diode across the LED if the input could see reversed polarity, since the LED only tolerates about 6 V backwards.",
