    how: "The chip runs your firmware plus a Bluetooth stack, the software that handles the protocol. The radio wakes for a few milliseconds, exchanges packets, then everything sleeps until the next event, so the average current can be a few microamps. A 32 MHz crystal provides the precise frequency the radio needs. An optional 32.768 kHz crystal keeps time during sleep; without it an internal RC oscillator works but must be recalibrated periodically and costs some extra power. An internal DC/DC converter (a tiny switching regulator) can replace the internal linear regulator, cutting radio current by roughly 40 %. Programming and debugging use SWD, a two-wire debug interface (SWDIO and SWCLK).",
