    summary: "A small three-pin sensor that detects a nearby magnet, either as an on/off switch or as a voltage proportional to field strength.",
    purpose: "Hall-effect sensors let a circuit notice a magnet without touching it. They count wheel or fan rotations, detect a lid or door being closed, find the home position of a motor, sense the position of a joystick or throttle, and measure current through the field it creates. They have no moving parts to wear out, unlike reed switches. Three types are common: the A3144 is a switch that turns on near a south pole; the SS49E is a linear sensor whose output voltage moves smoothly with field strength in both directions; the DRV5032FB is an ultra-low-power omnipolar switch (either pole works) that wakes up a few times a second and averages under 1 µA, ideal for battery devices.",
    how: "When current flows through a thin slab of semiconductor and a magnetic field passes through it, the field pushes the moving charges to one side, producing a tiny sideways voltage: the Hall effect, discovered in 1879. The chip amplifies this few-microvolt signal. In a linear sensor like the SS49E, the output rests at half the supply (about 2.5 V on 5 V) and rises or falls by roughly 1.4–1.8 mV per gauss (0.1 mT) depending on which pole approaches. In a switch, a built-in comparator with hysteresis flips the output when the field passes a threshold, typically a few to a few tens of millitesla, and releases at a slightly lower field so it does not chatter.",
