    summary: "Tiny MEMS sensors that measure acceleration, tilt and rotation; an IMU combines an accelerometer and gyroscope in one chip.",
    purpose: "An accelerometer measures acceleration along three axes, including the constant 1 g pull of gravity, so it can tell which way is down (tilt), detect taps, shakes, falls and steps, and measure vibration. A gyroscope measures rotation rate, how fast something is turning, in degrees per second. An IMU (inertial measurement unit) combines both, and software blends them to track orientation smoothly, as in drones, balancing robots, game controllers and phones. The ADXL335 is a simple analog ±3 g accelerometer; the ADXL345 is a digital ±2 to ±16 g accelerometer; the MPU-6050 and LSM6DS3 are 6-axis IMUs with accelerometer and gyroscope plus FIFO buffers and interrupt features.",
    how: "These are MEMS (micro-electro-mechanical systems) devices: microscopic silicon structures etched onto the chip. An accelerometer contains a tiny mass on springs between comb-like capacitor plates; when the chip accelerates, the mass lags behind, the gaps change, and the chip measures the change in capacitance. A gyroscope contains a vibrating structure; when rotated, the Coriolis effect pushes it sideways in proportion to the rotation rate. Both signals are tiny and noisy, so the chip amplifies, filters and (in digital parts) converts them. The output data rate (ODR) sets how many readings per second you get; higher ODR means more current and more noise bandwidth.",
    integration: "Digital sensors talk over I²C or SPI. On I²C, SDA and SCL need pull-up resistors (2.2–10 kΩ), and an address pin selects between two addresses: ADXL345 0x53 (SDO low) or 0x1D (high); MPU-6050 0x68 (AD0 low) or 0x69; LSM6DS3 0x6A or 0x6B (SA0). The bare chips run at 1.7–3.6 V; breakout boards like the GY-521 and GY-291 usually add a regulator and pull-ups. Decouple with 100 nF (plus the extra capacitors the datasheet lists, such as the MPU-6050's REGOUT capacitor). The INT pin can wake a sleeping microcontroller on motion or data ready. The analog ADXL335 outputs about 300 mV per g centred at half its supply; capacitors on the X, Y, Z outputs set its bandwidth.",
