    summary: "Small, cheap digital building blocks: the 74HC595 turns three microcontroller pins into eight outputs, and the 74HC14 cleans up messy signals.",
    purpose: "The 74HC series is a huge family of standard CMOS logic chips (gates, flip-flops, counters, multiplexers) that has been around since the 1980s. Two are especially handy alongside a microcontroller. The 74HC595 is a shift register: you clock eight bits in one at a time on a single data line, then latch them all to eight output pins at once, and chips can be chained for 16, 24 or more outputs, all from three pins. The 74HC14 contains six Schmitt-trigger inverters: each flips its input (high becomes low) and, crucially, has two different switching thresholds, so it turns slow, noisy or bouncy signals into crisp, clean edges.",
    how: "Inside the 74HC595 are two 8-bit rows of flip-flops (one-bit memories). On each rising edge of the shift clock (SRCLK, pin 11), every bit moves one place along and the data pin (SER, pin 14) enters the first position; the last bit leaves on QH' (pin 9) to feed the next chip. A rising edge on the latch clock (RCLK, pin 12) copies all eight bits to the output register, so outputs change together without flicker. In the 74HC14, positive feedback creates hysteresis: on 5 V the output switches as the input rises past about 2.7–3 V but does not switch back until it falls below about 1.6–1.8 V, so noise riding on the edge cannot cause multiple transitions.",
    integration: "74HC chips run from 2 V to 6 V. Put a 100 nF ceramic capacitor across VCC and GND of every package, right at the pins. For the 74HC595, tie SRCLR (pin 10, clear) high and OE (pin 13, output enable) low unless you need them; OE can take a PWM signal to dim all outputs. Each output can supply about 6 mA while staying within logic levels (absolute maximum 35 mA per pin, 70 mA total through VCC or GND), enough for LEDs with ~1 kΩ resistors. For debouncing a button with a 74HC14, use a 10 kΩ pull-up, a 10 kΩ series resistor and a 100 nF capacitor to ground at the input, giving clean single transitions.",
