    purpose: "A DC motor reverses when you reverse the voltage across it. Doing that from a single supply needs an H-bridge: four switches arranged like the letter H around the motor, closing diagonally opposite pairs to send current one way or the other. The L293D contains four half-bridges (two complete H-bridges) with logic inputs, so a microcontroller can control direction and, with PWM on the enable pins, speed. It handles motor supplies from 4.5 V to 36 V and about 600 mA per channel continuously (1.2 A peak). The ‘D’ version includes the clamp diodes needed for inductive loads. It is easy to use on a breadboard, though modern MOSFET drivers are more efficient.",
