    purpose: "Line-level audio and microcontroller sound outputs cannot drive a speaker: a speaker has a low resistance (typically 8 Ω) and needs current. The LM386 is a classic low-voltage power amplifier for exactly this. It runs from 4–12 V (the LM386N-4 version up to 18 V), idles at about 4 mA, and delivers around 0.3 W into 8 Ω at 6 V and roughly 0.5–0.7 W at 9 V. It is perfect for intercoms, small radios, guitar practice amps, toy sounds, alarm tones and making a project talk. It needs no split supply and few external parts, so it is often one of the first audio chips people build with.",
