    purpose: "Knowing how much current a circuit draws is vital for battery life estimates, solar panel logging, motor monitoring and spotting faults. The INA219 measures the small voltage across a low-value resistor (the shunt) in the supply line, and also the supply (bus) voltage, from 0 to 26 V. It converts both with its 12-bit ADC and calculates current and power internally, reporting them over I²C. The common module uses a 0.1 Ω shunt, giving a range of about ±3.2 A with steps of roughly 0.1–0.8 mA depending on configuration. It is ideal for measuring the power use of a project, a battery charger or a solar panel.",
    how: "Current flowing through the shunt resistor produces a proportional voltage, following Ohm's law: 1 A through 0.1 Ω gives 100 mV. The INA219 measures that voltage differentially between its VIN+ and VIN− pins, with a programmable full-scale range of ±40, 80, 160 or 320 mV, in steps of 10 µV. It separately measures the voltage at VIN− relative to ground (the bus voltage, in 4 mV steps). You write a calibration value based on your shunt resistance, and the chip then reports current directly in amps and multiplies by bus voltage to report power. Averaging up to 128 samples reduces noise.",
