    summary: "A tiny six-pin chip that clamps static discharges on USB data lines to ground before they reach and damage your microcontroller.",
    purpose: "Every time you touch a connector, you may deliver a static spark of several thousand volts. It lasts only nanoseconds but can quietly damage the delicate USB pins of a microcontroller, leading to devices that stop enumerating months later. The USBLC6-2SC6 is a low-capacitance ESD (electrostatic discharge) protection array for two data lines plus the 5 V supply, in a small SOT-23-6 package. It withstands ±8 kV contact and ±15 kV air discharges (the IEC 61000-4-2 test), and adds only a few picofarads to each line, so it works with USB 2.0 at full 480 Mbit/s high speed.",
    how: "Inside, each data line connects to a pair of fast steering diodes, one leading up to a common rail and one down to ground, and that rail is clamped by a Zener-like diode. In normal operation the diodes are off and invisible to signals between 0 and 5 V. When a static spike drives a line above the supply or below ground, a diode conducts in nanoseconds and steers the surge into the clamp and ground, holding the line to a voltage the protected chip can survive. The small diodes keep capacitance low, which matters because extra capacitance would blur fast USB signals.",
    integration: "Pinout: pins 1 and 6 are data line 1 (I/O1), pins 3 and 4 are data line 2 (I/O2), pin 2 is GND, pin 5 is VBUS. The paired pins allow a ‘flow-through’ layout: route D+ in on one pin and out on its partner, directly under the chip, with no stubs. Connect pin 5 to VBUS (5 V) and pin 2 to ground through a short, direct via to the ground plane. Place the chip as close as possible to the USB connector, so the surge is diverted before it travels along your board. Keep D+ and D− traces the same length and side by side (90 Ω differential) on both sides of the chip.",
