    purpose: "USB-C is now the standard way to power and connect small electronics: phones, microcontroller boards and gadgets. The receptacle on your board gives you 5 V power (VBUS), USB 2.0 data (D+ and D−), and two configuration channel pins (CC1 and CC2) that let the cable and charger figure out who is the power source and who is the device. Unlike older USB, a USB-C power source does not switch on its 5 V until it detects a device. Receptacles come in full 24-pin versions, simpler 16-pin USB 2.0 versions, and 6-pin power-only versions with just VBUS, GND, CC1 and CC2.",
    how: "The plug is symmetrical, so it fits either way up. The CC wires work out orientation and roles. A charger (source) has pull-up resistors on its CC pins; a device (sink) has 5.1 kΩ pull-down resistors to ground on each of CC1 and CC2. When a cable connects them, one CC line forms a voltage divider, the charger sees the resulting voltage, knows a device is present, and turns on VBUS. The pull-up value also tells the device how much current the charger offers: default USB power, 1.5 A or 3 A at 5 V. Higher voltages (9–20 V) require USB Power Delivery, a digital conversation over CC handled by a dedicated controller chip.",
    integration: "For a simple 5 V device: connect a separate 5.1 kΩ resistor from CC1 to ground and another from CC2 to ground. Connect all VBUS pins together and all GND pins together. For USB 2.0 data, the receptacle has two sets of D+ (A6, B6) and D− (A7, B7) pins, one for each plug orientation: join A6 to B6 and A7 to B7 with short traces, then run D+/D− as a 90 Ω differential pair through ESD protection to your microcontroller. Connect the metal shell to ground, directly or through a resistor and capacitor (for example 1 MΩ in parallel with 4.7 nF). Add input capacitance of 10 µF or less on VBUS, plus a TVS diode for surges.",
